course: dictionary
lesson: 7687
title: Protesters and context
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: A protester is someone who shows in public that they strongly disagree with something. Context helps you spot this. «Protesters marched through the town, holding signs about climate.» Marching, signs and climate all point to people speaking out in public, so a protester must be someone doing that.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «In 1773 protesters tipped British tea into Boston harbour.» The colonists were angry about being taxed without a say, so protesters must be people who were angry and acting on it. Does "people who were happy" fit? No, so that meaning is wrong.
explain: You may also see protestor spelled with an o. Both spellings are in the dictionary and mean the same thing. The verb is protest, as in people protest against a plan, and the event itself is also called a protest.
fact: A protester is a person who shows in public that they strongly disagree with something.
fact: Protesters often march, hold signs or shout to show what they think.
fact: «Protestor» is another spelling of the same word.
fact: Suffragettes were protesters who changed the law to win votes for women.
fact: In many countries, peaceful protest is a right.
exercise: «In 1773 protesters tipped British tea into Boston harbour.» What is a protester? | A ship that carries tea across the sea | *A person who shows in public they strongly disagree with something | A person who works at a harbour | A person who sells tea in a shop
exercise: Which of these is a protester doing? | Sleeping in the park | *Marching with a sign about climate | Reading a book quietly at home | Baking a cake for a school fair
exercise: Find and copy one word that means a person who shows strong disagreement in public. | painting | soup | *protesters | London
test: «Protesters marched outside the town hall, shouting about rising prices.» What were the protesters doing? | Selling vegetables at the market | Sleeping on the steps | Fixing the road | *Showing in public that they strongly disagree
test: Ella says a protester is someone who is just a bit cross at home, alone. Is she right? | Yes, anyone who is cross is a protester | Yes, a protester can be cross in private | *No, a protester shows their disagreement in public, not alone at home | No, a protester shows their disagreement in public
exam: Which sentence best explains what a protester is? | A protester is a type of tea sold in Boston | A protester is someone who always agrees with the government | A protester is a person who quietly works in an office all day | *A protester is a person who shows in public that they strongly disagree with something
